really punchy speech yesterday and you  said far too many conservatives in  england have forgotten that unionism is  in our party's dna  do you think that some of your  colleagues in westminster are just not  taking it seriously enough  well this was a wake-up call to the  party this was to remind everyone that  we are conservative  and unionists and the defeatism and  disinterest that i hear  too often sadly from colleagues from  members of the party  at south of the border is not helping  our fight up here because we are the  strongest  supporters for the union in scotland  we're the only ones that can stop the  snp  and put a break on their demands for  another independence referendum  to separate scotland but our message is  not being helped by those south of the  border  who somehow think this is a foregone  conclusion it's not we had a vote just  six years ago and two million scots  backed  scotland remaining part of the united  kingdom it's interesting  you talk about what people are saying  south of the border and in the speech  you also talk about  many including some who govern the  country want to see a uk government  focus on  england and you say that they're the  views of a small minority but i hear  them  far too often specifically what exactly  have you heard  well i think we've had a policy  and feeling amongst uk government  departments of devolving and forgetting  of passing on  barnet consequentials to scotland and  not looking at how that  benefits people right across the country  so i think it's to change that mindset  amongst government departments it sounds  like you're talking about specific  comments that you've heard from  other conservative mps it's it's an  attitude  it's a feeling that they pass on funding  significant funding through this covert  pandemic 6.5 billion pounds from the uk  government to the scottish government  and then they forget we need to do more  to remember that scotland has two  governments and the uk government and  the scottish government  should work together more to benefit  communities right across the country  so have you heard flipping comments  about the union from some of your  colleagues  well i'm not going to name colleagues  names i'm making this speech and it was  a stark speech it was to  make people look in the mirror and have  a long hard look at themselves to see  if they are doing enough to strengthen  the union have they done enough can they  do enough  should they do more and i think for a  number of people south of the border and  include the london media and the  commentary  who also seem to be writing this up as  if scotland it will somehow  become independent soon but they don't  look at the snp's record 13 years in  power here in scotland and our education  system  and standards are going backwards we  need to do far more to support our nhs  to get on top of crime to support the  local economy  because the national economy is  stagnating these are the issues we  should be focusing on  rather than working with a and for the  sepsis message to take scotland out of  the united kingdom  is boris johnson the man to save the  union  yes the prime minister is a strong  supporter of the united kingdom of the  union he believes that the  four nations of the united kingdom can  do far more together  than they can do separately on their own  but i think the prime minister would  also accept that his government and  successive governments  have not done enough to strengthen the  case for the union and that's why i made  my speech yesterday to put down this  marker to make sure everyone reflects on  what they have done and what they can do  to strengthen our four nations of the  united kingdom are his  approval ratings in scotland a problem  though  well approval ratings you know go up and  down and the future of our country a  300-year  economic political partnership well i  was just saying sophie  a 300-year political and economic  partnership that has served scotland  in the uk well doesn't rest on if one  individual is popular or not  this is about what our country means and  what we can do together united  as one nation working together  now i'm keen to talk to you about  margaret ferrier the  former snp mp who took that train from  glasgow to london  and then travelled back to glasgow after  receiving news that she tested positive  for covert 19. i mean how surprised  are you that she hasn't resigned yet i'm  surprised amazed and shocked that she  hasn't resigned she has  no credibility as a member of parliament  anymore people look to mps to set a  standard to follow the guidelines  that mps themselves have assisted in  making  and her actions were irresponsible they  were dangerous and they have endangered  many people she came into contact with  back to when she had symptoms over a  week ago to when she traveled to london  last monday knowing that she'd had a  test and then when she traveled on  public transport on a train from london  to glasgow  on tuesday having previously had a  positive test result for cover 19 the  night before  every element of that story has  endangered people right across the  country and she must stand down as a  member of parliament  now of course you resigned as a junior  minister when dominic cummings  broke the coronavirus rules  how damaging is it when people like  margaret ferrier when people like  dominic cummings as well  don't follow the same rules that  everyone else is supposed to  well i'm not here to support dominic  cummings i couldn't support him back in  may that's why i resigned my position  within the uk government but the case  with margaret ferrier  is extremely different and more serious  because she knowingly traveled  she got onto public transport with other  people on a five hour plus train journey  from london to glasgow knowing that she  was infectious knowing that she could  pass that virus on  to others she came into contact with a  virus we're still trying to get on top  of here in scotland and across the uk  that's still taking lives just this week  we've seen the highest daily death total  here in scotland for some time we are  still in the middle of this pandemic and  her actions  have endangered the safety of so many  people and i do have to question why  if nicola sturgeon thinks she's not fit  to be an mp anymore why does nicola  sturgeon think margaret ferrier  should still be in the snp still be a  member of that party  at the same time though talking about  the snp reaction nicholas sturgeon's  response to margaret farah has been  far far stronger than the conservative  response to dominic cummings she's been  stripped to the whip  nicholas sturgeon has spoke to her  saying she should stand down as an mp  you were the only person to actually  quit over dominic cummings do you think  in retrospect the conservatives should  have been much stronger  well i can only speak for my own actions  and i as you say quit the uk government  because i couldn't defend  what dominic cummings had done but i do  think there are questions on the  timeline here from the snp  when did they know about it did margaret  ferry or not tell a single colleague or  a single person in the snp  that she'd taken a test that she'd  tested positive that she'd traveled from  london to scotland with that positive  test  why did the commons authorities know  about it on wednesday afternoon  but the first minister and leader of the  snp didn't know about it until thursday  lunchtime  why did it take time for the whip to be  withdrawn they agreed a statement with  margaret ferrier and at the initial  stages they weren't withdrawing the whip  and as i say  nicola sturgeon believes margaret  ferrier shouldn't be a member of  parliament but she's not said  why she should continue as a member of  the scottish national party
 

